Yes—and this is where the distinction between genealogy and resonance becomes useful.

Divine, define, and defy occupy a striking English sound-neighborhood, but historically they are not one etymological family. Divine comes through Latin divinus, connected with god/heaven and ultimately a PIE root associated with brightness/sky; define comes from Latin definire, literally involving setting a boundary or limit; defy comes through Old French from a root involving withdrawing faith/trust or allegiance.  Yet once English puts /dɪˈvaɪn/, /dɪˈfaɪn/, and /dɪˈfaɪ/ into the same cognitive neighborhood, speakers can create associations that their historical origins never contained. That secondary relationship is culturally real even though it is not etymological.

And the verbs accidentally make a beautiful three-way operator:

\textbf{divine}=\text{discern/infer}

\textbf{define}=\text{draw the boundary}

\textbf{defy}=\text{cross/refuse the boundary}

Interestingly, English divine itself acquired a non-supernatural sense of making something out through observation or conjecture, so even historically the word wandered toward the territory we’re discussing. 

Now do your cross-language test. If that three-way relationship vanishes when translated into Mandarin, Arabic, Navajo, Swahili, etc., then part of what we’re seeing is an English-specific phonological affordance. But if analogous conceptual relationships keep reappearing despite completely different sounds, then we may be approaching deeper primitives. Those primitives needn’t be mystical or even linguistic. They might be things like:

\text{boundary},\quad
\text{inside/outside},\quad
\text{trust/betrayal},\quad
\text{agency/resistance},\quad
\text{known/unknown},\quad
\text{light/dark},\quad
\text{self/other}

Those are good candidates because human bodies, perception, social life, and environments repeatedly generate them. Different languages could carve the same underlying experiential geometry into very different words.

That gives us a serious discovery instrument. Overlay etymology + phonology + semantic change + geography + trade/migration + religion + technological change + ecology + cognitive universals. Then look for correlations while running controls against coincidence. A sound resemblance in one language is weak evidence; the same semantic transformation independently appearing across twenty unrelated languages is much more interesting. Conversely, the same word spreading along an ancient trade route tells a historical story rather than necessarily revealing a universal human primitive.

You can even formulate the search as:

\text{surface words}
\rightarrow
\text{semantic relations}
\rightarrow
\text{cross-language invariants}
\rightarrow
\text{historical overlay}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate primitive}
\rightarrow
\text{independent test}

That would let us mine language not merely for etymology but as a kind of fossil record of cognition—with the important caveat that languages are full of accidents, borrowing, convergence, puns, and retrospective stories. The false positives are part of why the verification layer we’ve been building matters.

And on your Holy Spirit line: as a metaphor for transmission, inspiration, understanding, and something moving through multiple perspectives, I see the connection you’re making. Theologically, though, mainstream Nicene Christianity would not say God made the Holy Spirit; Father, Son, and Holy Spirit are understood as uncreated and coeternal. So “the Holy Spirit as the connective/discovery principle” is an interesting interpretive analogy, rather than the standard doctrinal account of why the Spirit exists.

There is a nice recursive twist here too: language itself can be both our archaeological site and our archaeological tool. We use patterns in language to discover the patterns that caused language—and then use those discoveries to look back underneath language again.